{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"f41e76e96781f4d1f396f88ac8690f5f40b6d1f366f1ae626c526b8f1fcdcedc","slug":"introduction","location":"/introduction.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Introduction","short_title":"✅ Introduction","content_includes_title":false,"thumbnail":"/playbook/pr-preview/pr-9/build/umbrella_logo-a3fe9e0d5aa24633556bae999be918c4.svg","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"introduction.md","url":"/playbook/pr-preview/pr-9/build/introduction-f870ce5869accf0399f697b32cf011ac.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/playbook/pr-preview/pr-9/build/umbrella_logo-a3fe9e0d5aa24633556bae999be918c4.svg","alt":"The UC OSPO Network logo, modified so that it appears to be holding an umbrella.","width":"70%","align":"center","key":"D36sim0Cet","urlSource":"images/umbrella_logo.svg"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open source software (OSS) is ubiquitous in academia, just as it is ubiquitous in every layer of our digital lives. It’s been ","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"CwdxErIlDn"},{"type":"link","url":"https://todogroup.org/blog/state-of-ospo-2024/","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"estimated","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"c7UNQiuA6s"}],"urlSource":"https://todogroup.org/blog/state-of-ospo-2024/","key":"MqkRns646L"},{"type":"text","value":" that 94% of companies use open source software, all major programming languages are available in open source implementations, and as you read this document right now, you’re using OSS, too.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"iTjT2XkW1O"}],"key":"LlssyxV6Jc"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The remarkable success of OSS, however, belies a crisis under the surface. A large proportion of open source maintainers are overworked, underpaid, and ","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"lVad3x3ASd"},{"type":"link","url":"https://www.sonarsource.com/the-2024-tidelift-maintainer-impact-report.pdf","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"battling burnout","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"bgeizcmi5Z"}],"urlSource":"https://www.sonarsource.com/the-2024-tidelift-maintainer-impact-report.pdf","key":"C3pCVvd30K"},{"type":"text","value":", even those who are maintaining projects with great utility. Academic open source maintainers are no exception--surveys show that the majority of research software contributors lack sufficient funding to support software development activities for their research (","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"V4svyKE6B8"},{"type":"cite","url":"https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj-cs.963","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Carver et al. 2022","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"zLkONr45gy"}],"kind":"narrative","label":"Carver_2022","identifier":"https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj-cs.963","enumerator":"1","key":"ZRc9GtHAr3"},{"type":"text","value":"), and that they believe the most useful support a university could provide for their software development work would be grants for sustainability (","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"WPVYhzbbxV"},{"type":"link","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal/pone.0348894","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scarlett et al. 2026","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"Jjutmju2Es"}],"urlSource":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal/pone.0348894","data":{"doi":"10.1371/journal/pone.0348894"},"internal":false,"protocol":"doi","key":"PfroJSUdQT"},{"type":"text","value":"). Addressing this crisis on a global scale will take time, but there are things that you can do now to strengthen your project’s financial and social future.","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"KuJy4aHKse"}],"key":"qImMaf7Yt7"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This playbook is for contributors, creators, and maintainers of academic open source software (OSS) at the University of California. We’ll discuss the strategies that UC OSS projects have used to sustain success, ways that UC was a partner on the journey, and some ways in which UC is still catching up to the fast-moving world of OSS. Hardware creators will also find useful content here, though the focus is on software (hardware creators may want to also check out ","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"jS2Qm9cOBU"},{"type":"link","url":"https://ouch-advice.github.io/","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"https://​ouch​-advice​.github​.io/","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"k8MulpNYr9"}],"urlSource":"https://ouch-advice.github.io/","key":"RVA6yt0OpC"},{"type":"text","value":"). Whether you’re a faculty member, staff, or student, if you’re interested in how open source projects do more with less, this playbook is for you.","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"IFCTmsuosR"}],"key":"sG6pjM3Q2F"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Various definitions of open source sustainability have been proposed (e.g. ","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"rOwGxAGlLQ"},{"type":"link","url":"https://osaos-book.codeforscience.org/","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"OSAOS","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"W8uZmuoJSY"}],"urlSource":"https://osaos-book.codeforscience.org/","key":"WT3uNMDICf"},{"type":"text","value":", ","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"KYpxk1KGRJ"},{"type":"link","url":"https://danielskatzblog.wordpress.com/2018/09/26/fundamentals-of-software-sustainability/","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Daniel Katz","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"igu6a8XYRc"}],"urlSource":"https://danielskatzblog.wordpress.com/2018/09/26/fundamentals-of-software-sustainability/","key":"MXUo2vyifZ"},{"type":"text","value":", ","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"aE75nXN826"},{"type":"link","url":"https://sustainoss.org/about/","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"SustainOSS","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"ldCHUDXGa2"}],"urlSource":"https://sustainoss.org/about/","key":"Nn6fvJGhlW"},{"type":"text","value":"), but the gist of them is this: a sustainable project is a living thing, adaptable, persistent, and resilient to challenge or uncertainty. As technology and user needs evolve, a sustainable project resists obsolescence, and either stays relevant or grows increasingly useful to a community of users and contributors. This playbook is designed to help you cultivate a sustainability mindset–keeping an eye out for opportunities, and making time for activities with long-term payoffs.","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"bvjn5UEBwX"}],"key":"rkTRD2Sqgy"},{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A sustainable project is a living thing, adaptable, persistent, and resilient to challenge or uncertainty.","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"i3m7GydNjI"}],"key":"wsGnrlQwq6"}],"key":"oXeooXKYLP"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Many academics may understandably feel that sustainability means funding. However, plenty of well-funded projects have fizzled out, and plenty of scrappy projects have achieved remarkable success. In OSS, as the saying goes, money isn’t everything. We offer a holistic approach to sustainability, inspired by the four facets presented in the ","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"olOmdglhUP"},{"type":"link","url":"https://itav.lyrasis.org/guidebook/","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"It Takes a Village Guidebook by Lyrasis","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"Fn22o8Bk90"}],"urlSource":"https://itav.lyrasis.org/guidebook/","key":"pNaxHFTTJo"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"LxE5305CJP"}],"key":"TUG4I1l6Rj"},{"type":"image","url":"/playbook/pr-preview/pr-9/build/facets_new-17fd0dadacbea995609dd4fd4d8776ec.svg","alt":"A diagram showing the four facets of sustainability with decorative pictures above each.","width":"100%","align":"center","class":"dark:hidden","key":"GwTbedk1Rx","urlSource":"images/facets_new.svg"},{"type":"image","url":"/playbook/pr-preview/pr-9/build/facets_new_dark-3a3b09788b35731d80f719cad42e33f6.svg","alt":"A diagram showing the four facets of sustainability with decorative pictures above each.","width":"100%","align":"center","class":"hidden dark:block","key":"hr6SQfRLxk","urlSource":"images/facets_new_dark.svg"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Discoverability:","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"b03yx0oRP1"}],"key":"oKNhC4lGuN"},{"type":"text","value":" Provide records that will make your software easier to find and use.","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"G2gRTVRI3e"}],"key":"FyNnA5umBl"}],"key":"D0MGF9a3Im"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Funding:","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"key":"WGbJkMfOkV"}],"key":"Ht5e3ox7MD"},{"type":"text","value":" Consider four key mechanisms for funding for academic open source: sponsored projects; entrepreneurship; gifts, non-profits, and fiscal sponsorship; and institutional funding.","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"key":"rJFzaJVdAs"}],"key":"V5UpFl0cK6"}],"key":"P586zm6t1k"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Community:","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"nmD5iKVawp"}],"key":"aI4OnNZpk1"},{"type":"text","value":" Build a welcoming community to fuel innovation, recognition, and trust.","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"y0eXRH4Kvf"}],"key":"WC9YvGQZin"}],"key":"WsVoYaSyH9"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Product design:","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"oEaXt04gFN"}],"key":"Z2E0WMwKki"},{"type":"text","value":" Encourage community contributions by building a product that is designed to be modified.","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"W2ro5hvScj"}],"key":"FQgpIMOen4"}],"key":"hLiWsv4Ye4"}],"key":"bVVFviSq2r"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Academic open source creators are a diverse group. To reflect their varieties of experience, we interviewed UC researchers who have found innovative ways to sustain (or thoughtfully sunset) their OSS at UC, and incorporated the interviews into this book as quotes, anecdotes, and ideas. Abridged transcripts of the full interviews are available in the appendix. Sustaining OSS at UC may feel daunting, but you are not alone. There are other researchers who have navigated these hurdles already, and we’ve collected their stories here for you.","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"CHuybiah89"}],"key":"hA2ICtlDlU"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure